<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Coordinates of the stationary points of a cubic</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the coordinates of the two stationary points of the curve \(y = {@f@}\). Give the point with the smaller \(x\)-coordinate first.</p><div>First point: ( [[input:ans1]] , [[input:ans2]] ) [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><div>Second point: ( [[input:ans3]] , [[input:ans4]] ) [[validation:ans3]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>At a stationary point the tangent is horizontal: the curve is momentarily neither rising nor falling, so \(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 0\).</p><p>\[\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@df@} = {@dfac@}\]This is zero when \(x = {@p0@}\) or \(x = {@q0@}\).</p><p>When \(x = {@p0@}\), \(y = {@yp@}\). When \(x = {@q0@}\), \(y = {@yq@}\).</p><p>The stationary points are \(({@p0@}, {@yp@})\) and \(({@q0@}, {@yq@})\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
